   "source": [
    "<div id=\"p2-snap\" style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif; color:#1a1a1a; max-width: 900px; line-height:1.5;\">\n",
    "<h1>Localised Precision-Weighted Ensembles for Crop Yield Prediction</h1>\n",
    "<p style=\"color:#555; margin-top:0; font-size:18px;\">Farm-level adaptive weighting across a multi-model ensemble, 35,000+ Indian smallholder farms</p>\n",
    "<div>\n",
    "  <span style=\"display:inline-block; padding:3px 10px; border-radius:999px; font-size:12px; font-weight:600; margin-right:6px; margin-bottom:6px; background:#eaf2ff; color:#1854c4;\">Ensemble ML</span>\n",
    "  <span style=\"display:inline-block; padding:3px 10px; border-radius:999px; font-size:12px; font-weight:600; margin-right:6px; margin-bottom:6px; background:#fff3e6; color:#b5570a;\">Prediction Uncertainty</span>\n",
    "  <span style=\"display:inline-block; padding:3px 10px; border-radius:999px; font-size:12px; font-weight:600; margin-right:6px; margin-bottom:6px; background:#fff2a9; color:#b5578a;\">Model Agnosticism</span>\n",
    "  <span style=\"display:inline-block; padding:3px 10px; border-radius:999px; font-size:12px; font-weight:600; margin-right:6px; margin-bottom:6px; background:#fff1a9; color:#b3342a;\">Error Adaptation</span>\n",
    "  <span style=\"display:inline-block; padding:3px 10px; border-radius:999px; font-size:12px; font-weight:600; margin-right:6px; margin-bottom:6px; background:#eafbf1; color:#0a8a4a;\">SHAP Interpretability</span>\n",
    "</div>\n",
    "</div>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "2de16737",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "<div style=\"font-size: 1.5em; line-height: 1.3;font-family: 'Sitka Banner', 'Sitka Text', Georgia, serif; text-align:center; width:100%;\">\n",
    "<div> Sukanya Mukherjee</div>\n",
    "<div style=\"font-size: 0.75em; line-height: 1.3;font-family: 'Sitka Banner', 'Sitka Text', Georgia, serif; text-align:center; width:100%;\">\n",
    "IWH Halle (Saale) and MLU Halle (Saale)\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "97fabf80",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "<div style=\"font-size: 1.3em; line-height: 1.4; font-family: 'Sitka Banner', 'Sitka Text', Georgia, serif; text-align: justify; width: 80%; margin: 20px auto; padding: 20px 25px; border: 1px solid #ccc; background-color: #fafafa; border-radius: 4px;\">\n",
    "    <div style=\"margin: 0;\"><b>Abstract.</b> <i> Nothing is true... </i> Multi-model prediction tensors present the problem of model-choice. This paper circumvents this problem with a localised precision-weighting ensemble framework that aggregates signals across diverse inductive biases and cross-validation folds to generate robust point-predictions. The final point estimator predicts yields for rice and wheat farms with competitive accuracies, $R^2=0.54$ and $R^2=0.59$, respectively. \n",
    "\n",
    "<i> Everything is permitted... </i> This paper prioritizes robustness over model- or fold-level outperformance due to the heterogeneity inherent in Indian agriculture. The candidate models with higher accuracy have higher weight in the ensemble estimator, simultaneously, models are penalized if they over-fit. Empirical results suggest that the given set of models -- Random Forest, XGBoost and Support Vector Regression -- have homoscedastic prediction risk, consequently the arithmetic mean is the robust estimator. \n",
    "\n",
    "A ``sanity-check'', based on Stability Ratio, ensures that farm-level predictions are dominated by between-model diversity rather than within-model sampling noise; 98\\% farms qualify this reliability threshold.\n",
    "\n",
    "The prediction residuals are heteroscedastic, as seen in the U-shaped error distribution at yield tails. This is exploited to identify farms with high tail-risk, using conditional quantile prediction intervals. Applying dispersion weighting to this subsample of high prediction uncertainty farms, improves point-prediction $R^2$ by 10\\%. Finally, Shapley-based feature importance is used to highlight the relative importance of climate conditions, input costs and farm-management. Together, this creates a comprehensive farm-level profile that integrates robust median forecasts with explicit measures of risk exposure and feature influence, that can potentially inform agricultural policy and risk-management.</div>\n"
   ]
  },
